<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#cathedral">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cathedral</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A cathedral is a church that contains the cathedra, the seat of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, and so serves as the central church of a diocese, mainly in churches with an episcopal hierarchy such as the Catholic, Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a>, Anglican and some Lutheran churches. Churches serving as cathedrals first appeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> in the 4th century; among the earliest with visible remains is the complex below the Cathedral of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquileia">Aquileia</a>, dated between 313 and 319. They became universal in the Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> by the 12th century, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> many in presbyterian lands kept the title without a bishop.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word comes from the French cathédrale, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> ecclesia cathedralis and cathedra, the seat. The chair, raised above clergy and laity, marked a teacher and a presiding magistrate in the ancient world, so it symbolises the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>'s role as teacher and governor of his diocese. Strictly there could be no cathedrals before the Peace of the Church in 313, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine the Great</a> adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, since before the 4th century bishops presided standing on a raised platform. A Christian house church buried at Dura Europos in 256 had a hall for 60 to 75 standing and a platform too low for a throne.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Four functions have remained distinctive since the first cathedrals: a regular cycle of choral prayer, a forum for civic leadership, a commitment to higher learning and the promotion of music. A secular cathedral chapter normally had four dignitaries besides the canons: the dean, precentor, chancellor and treasurer. The building is often oriented east and west with a cross-shaped plan, whose long arm is the nave, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> for ship. Most cathedrals hold at least three services a day, and some, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a>, were traditional coronation places of monarchs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>In the 8th century Chrodegang, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>, compiled a rule for cathedral clergy to live under a common roof, a modification of the Benedictine rule. Gisa, bishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">Wells</a> from 1061 to 1088, introduced it into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, but it was not followed for long. Cathedrals did not become universal in the Western Catholic Church until the 12th century. In the English Reformation Henry VIII dissolved the monastic cathedral chapters and, except for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bath">Bath</a> and Coventry, refounded them as chapters of canons headed by a dean. In Lutheran lands churches such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nidaros-cathedral">Nidaros Cathedral</a> and Lübeck Cathedral became the seats of Protestant bishops.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cathedral” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
